  "preview": "Dre flipped a water bottle 60 times and landed it 27 times. What does that say about the 'real' probability? Compare experimental data to theoretical models, run a simulation, and decide whose claim to trust.",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Distinguish experimental probability (what actually happened) from theoretical probability (what the model predicts), and develop the intuition that experimental probability gets closer to theoretical as the number of trials grows (the Law of Large Numbers, informally).",
    "studentPrompt": "Three friends had an argument in the lunchroom about water-bottle flipping.\n\n- Dre flipped a half-full water bottle 60 times. It landed upright 27 times.\n- Priya flipped her own bottle 12 times. It landed upright 8 times.\n- Marcus said, \"Bottle flipping is 50-50. It either lands or it doesn't.\"\n\n1. Find the experimental probability of landing upright for Dre and for Priya. Express each as a fraction and a percent (round to the nearest whole percent).\n\n2. Is Marcus's reasoning correct? Use the words 'equally likely' in your answer.\n\n3. Whose experimental probability would you trust more as an estimate of the 'true' probability of landing the bottle: Dre's or Priya's? Why?\n\n4. Suppose Dre's experimental probability is close to the truth. About how many landings would you expect in 500 flips? In 1,000 flips?\n\n5. Run a SIMULATION. Use a spinner, a 10-sided die, or a random number generator set so that 'landing' happens with probability 45%. Do 20 trials. Record your number of landings. Combine results across the class. What do you notice as the total number of trials grows?",
    "image": {
      "kind": "url",
      "url": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/31699476/pexels-photo-31699476.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
      "alt": "Focused view of three clear plastic bottles with green caps outdoors.",
      "credit": "Photo by Patrick on Pexels"
    },
    "launch": "Before showing the prompt, ask the room: 'What do you think the chance is that a water bottle flip lands?' Take guesses. Write three or four on the board. Now show Dre and Priya's data. 'Now what do you think?' Send them to the boards.\n\nIf you have time and bottles, do real flips in Q5 instead of a simulation.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "10-sided dice OR spinners with 20 sectors OR random number generator (1-100)",
      "Optional: actual half-full water bottles for live data"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "experimental-probability",
      "theoretical-probability",
      "sample-size",
      "simulation",
      "expected-value",
      "law-of-large-numbers"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Excellent BTC. Q1 is fraction calculation (low floor). Q2 corners students on the common 'either it does or it doesn't = 50-50' misconception. Q3 is the conceptual diamond — most students sense Dre's data is 'more' but cannot articulate why. Q5 builds Law of Large Numbers experientially through pooled class data.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: Dre: 27/60 = 9/20 = 45%. Priya: 8/12 = 2/3 ~ 67%.\n- Q2: Wrong. 'Either-or' does not make outcomes equally likely. Counter-example: 'I either win the lottery or I don't' is not 50-50.\n- Q3: Dre. Larger sample = more trustworthy estimate. Some groups will say Priya because her percentage is higher — push them on whether 'higher' means 'truer.'\n- Q4: 500 x 0.45 = 225 landings. 1000 x 0.45 = 450 landings.\n- Q5: Each group gets ~9 landings out of 20 (close to but rarely exactly 45%). Combined class data of 400+ trials usually lands within a few percent of 45%.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- 'Two outcomes means 50-50.' Pervasive and worth dedicated discussion time. The 'lottery' counter-example or 'I will or won't get hit by lightning today' cracks it.\n- Trusting Priya because her percentage 'feels right' or is closer to 50%. The diamond move is recognizing sample size matters, regardless of the value the percentage takes.\n- Treating one group's 20-trial simulation as 'the answer' rather than data to pool. Resist the urge to summarize too early — let the pooled data tell the story.\n- For Q4, computing 500 / 0.45 instead of 500 x 0.45.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Marcus's 'either-or' argument: why does it FEEL right? Where exactly does it break?\n- A group used Priya's 67% to predict landings in Q4. Another group used Dre's 45%. Which prediction would you bet your lunch money on?\n- After the simulation, look at the pooled class total. How close is it to 45%? How close was each individual group? What does that pattern suggest?\n- If we flipped Dre's bottle 10,000 times and still got an experimental probability of 45%, would you say the 'true' probability IS 45%? Or could it still be wrong?",
    "extensions": "- A different student, Lin, flipped a bottle 300 times and landed 132. Combine Lin's data with Dre's into one big experimental probability. Is the combined estimate more or less trustworthy than either alone? Why?\n- Design a simulation for a DIFFERENT real-world event whose true probability you don't know exactly (e.g., a free throw, a coin landing on its edge, your bus being late). How would you set it up? What's your stopping rule?\n- A carnival game lets you flip a bottle. If it lands, you win $5. If not, you pay $2. Using Dre's 45% estimate, would you play? Compute the expected gain or loss per play.",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Dre: 27/60 = 9/20 = 45%. Priya: 8/12 = 2/3 ~ 67%.\n\n2. Marcus is wrong. 'Either it lands or it doesn't' identifies the two possible outcomes but does not make them equally likely. Equally likely means each outcome has the same chance. A bottle is much more likely to flop than to land, so the two outcomes are not equally likely.\n\n3. Dre's. With 60 trials, his experimental probability is much more stable. Priya's 12 flips could easily get 8 landings by luck even if the true probability is around 45%. Larger samples produce estimates closer to the truth.\n\n4. Using ~45%:\n  - 500 flips: 500 x 0.45 = 225 landings expected\n  - 1000 flips: 1000 x 0.45 = 450 landings expected\n  Both are estimates, not guarantees.\n\n5. Each group's 20-trial result will scatter (often 6 to 12 landings, sometimes more extreme). The pooled class data of several hundred trials typically lands within ~3 percentage points of 45%. The pattern: experimental probability gets closer to theoretical as the number of trials grows.",
